config TYPEC_TPS6598X_CORE
	tristate
	select USB_ROLE_SWITCH
	select POWER_SUPPLY
	select USB_COMMON if DEBUG_FS

config TYPEC_TPS6598X
	tristate "TI TPS6598x USB Power Delivery controller driver"
	depends on I2C
	select REGMAP_I2C
	select TYPEC_TPS6598X_CORE
	help
	  Say Y or M here if your system has TI TPS65982 or TPS65983 USB Power
	  Delivery controller.

	  If you choose to build this driver as a dynamically linked module, the
	  module will be called tps6598x.ko.

config TYPEC_SN201202X
	tristate "TI SN201202x USB Power Delivery controller support"
	depends on SPMI
	select TYPEC_TPS6598X_CORE
	help
	  Say Y here to enable support for SN201202x, a TPS6598x variant
	  that uses SPMI as a host bus, and is found on Apple devices starting
	  from the M3 series.

	  If you choose to build this driver as a dynamically linked module, the
	  module will be called sn201202x.ko.

config TYPEC_TPS6598X_DEBUG_COMMANDS
	bool "Debugfs command access to TPS6598x/CD321x controllers"
	depends on TYPEC_TPS6598X_CORE && DEBUG_FS
	help
	  Add a root-only debugfs file, usb/tipd/<device>/command, that sends
	  a few controller commands (LOCK, DBMa, VDMs, DVEn and DISC) to the
	  USB Power Delivery controller. Tools such as tuxvdmtool use it to
	  send Apple VDMs (reboot, DFU, serial, Debug USB) to a Mac connected
	  to the port, or to enable the local end of a debug route.

	  These commands can disconnect the port or reboot the connected
	  device. The read-only register file, usb/tipd/<device>/regs, is
	  available with DEBUG_FS whether or not this is set.

	  Say N unless you use the controller's debug mode for development.

config TYPEC_TIPD_KUNIT_TEST
	tristate "KUnit tests for CD321x" if !KUNIT_ALL_TESTS
	depends on KUNIT && TYPEC_TPS6598X_CORE
	select REGMAP
	default KUNIT_ALL_TESTS
	help
	  Test suspend gating, fresh cable-state revalidation, bounded retries
	  and removal during resume. The tests exercise the state transitions
	  shared with the driver, including aborted and repeated sleep cycles.
	  Also test that interrupt events which could not be read or cleared
	  are still acknowledged, however long that keeps failing, so that the
	  controller keeps raising its interrupt.
	  Also test that a USB4/Thunderbolt router which never registers its
	  switch does not keep the port from probing.
	  Also test that a Type-C state which a mux provider with transient
	  timeouts holds up is applied again until it goes through, for up to
	  about two minutes, also next to a display route that declares
	  nothing, and that other providers and errors keep the bounded
	  retries.
	  They also cover which DisplayPort hotplug event a cable update sends
	  to the display connector.
	  These tests run without accessing a physical Type-C controller.

config TYPEC_SN201202X_KUNIT_TEST
	tristate "KUnit tests for SN201202x" if !KUNIT_ALL_TESTS
	depends on KUNIT && TYPEC_SN201202X && SUSPEND
	select REGMAP
	default KUNIT_ALL_TESTS
	help
	  Test that the SN201202x events are not handled while the controller
	  sleeps, that a system suspend that fails leaves the controller awake,
	  and that an idle controller sleeps and wakes for register access and
	  for system suspend. Also test which data status and status count as
	  a link that wakes the system from suspend-to-idle. These tests run on
	  a simulated SPMI bus, without accessing a physical Type-C controller.
